WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.782
The first lab step creates a small dataframe.

00:00:02.782 --> 00:00:06.357
Read the variable names before focusing on the code syntax.

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:08.487
Patient ID is an identifier.

00:00:08.487 --> 00:00:11.902
Triage level and membership tier are ordered categories.

00:00:11.902 --> 00:00:13.827
Temperature is an interval measurement.

00:00:13.827 --> 00:00:16.007
ER visits is a ratio count.

00:00:16.007 --> 00:00:19.367
Run this in Jupyter, Colab, or VS Code.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:25.267
We are deliberately using a tiny dataset so you can inspect the values manually while learning the concepts.

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:29.707
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:35.327
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:37.532
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:43.842
In a real setting such as a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity,

00:00:43.842 --> 00:00:48.472
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:50.972
before discussing syntax.

00:00:50.972 --> 00:00:56.240
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
